# Chapter 1: The ear

## Anatomy and physiology

## Symptoms and signs

## Clinical testing of hearing, and audiometry

- **Whisper and voice test** - not routinely performed as they are subjective and require a controlled quiet environment
- **Tuning fork tests** - distinguish between conductive and sensorineural hearing loss:
  - **Tuning forks** - 256-512 Hz forks used for both Rinne and Weber test, sounded by striking the tines against elbow
  - **Rinne test** - compares AC (via ear canal and middle ear) and BC (direct transmission via mastoid process):
    - <u>Technique</u>:
      - 1\. Sound the fork and place on mastoid to test BC
      - 2\. When BC cannot be heard, place fork by ear to test AC (normally, patient detects return of sound)
      - 3\. Alternatively, compare loudness of BC and AC (normally AC \> BC)
    - <u>Interpretation</u>:
      - **Rinne positive** (normal) - AC better than bone conduction
      - **Rinne negative** (CHL) - BC better than AC
  - **Weber test** - more sensitive than Rinne's test:
    - <u>Technique</u>:
      - 1\. Sound the fork and place on the forehead in the midline (sound waves transmitted equally to both ears)
    - <u>Interpretation</u>:
      - Lateralisation towards side w/ hearing loss - CHL
      - Lateralisation towards opposite side - SNHL

    
    ![](img/rinne_weber.png)
- **Pure tone audiometry** - standard of recording hearing levels:
  - <u>Technique</u> - measurement of thresholds of hearing (in dB) at each tone (i.e. frequency) via air and bone conduction, against a reference of normal threshold for hearing in young adults
  - <u>Interpretation</u>:
    - **Severity of hearing loss** - arbitrarily defined based on hearing threshold as mild (20-40 dB), moderate (40-70dB), severe (70-90 dB) or profound (\> 90 dB)
    - **Type of hearing loss:**
      - <u>Conductive hearing loss</u> (CHL) - normal BC with air-bone gap \> 10 dB
      - <u>Sensorineural hearing loss</u> (SNHL) - increased threshold for BC and AC at higher frequencies w/o AB gap
      - <u>Mixed</u> - increased threshold for BC and AC w/ AB gap

    
    ![](img/pure_tone_audiogram.png)

## Investigations

## Hearing loss

- **Classification of hearing loss:**
  - <u>Conductive hearing loss</u> (CHL) - disease of the outer or middle ear
  - <u>Sensorineural hearing loss</u> (SNHL) - disease affecting the cochlea, CN VIII or the temporal lobe
- **Etiology of hearing loss:** 
![](img/etiology_of_hearing_loss.png)
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - <u>HPI</u> - onset, laterality, and rate of progression of hearing loss
  - <u>Associated Sx</u>:
    - Otalgia
    - Otorrhoea
    - Tinnitus
    - Imbalance
  - <u>Risk factors</u>:
    - SHx - excessive noise exposure (e.g. occupational)
    - Drug Hx - ototoxic agents
    - FHx
- 

## Otalgia

- **Definition** - earache
- **Pathophysiology of otalgia:**
  - <u>Pain detected within ear</u> - due to pathological processes occuring in the external ear, EAC, or tympanic membrane
  - <u>Referred pain</u> - pain referred via cranial nerves supplying external or middle ear
- **Etiology of otalgia** - classified into otological or non-otalgia causes:
  - **Otological causes:**
    - <u>Infective</u>:
      - External ear pathologies - e.g. Perichondritis, Acute otitis externa, malignant otitis externa, myringitis bullosa
      - Middle ear pathologies - Acute otitis media
      - Ramsey Hunt syndrome
    - <u>Acute otitic barotrauma</u>
    - <u>Neoplasms of the ear</u> - either via nerve impingement or causing perichondritis
  - **Non-otological causes** - referred pain from several cranial nerves (V, IX, X), and posterior roots of C2-3:
    - <u>Referred pain from CN V</u> - nasal and paranasal sinus pathologies, TMJ, parotid gland, oral cavity
    - <u>Referred pain from CN IX</u> - oropharynx
    - <u>Referred pain from CN X</u> - larynx and hypopharynx, oesophagus

## Otorrhoea

- **Definition** - an aural discharge, mary arise from disease of the ear canal, but is more commonly a/w middle ear infections
- **Etiology of otorrhoea:**
  - <u>External ear pathologies</u> - acute and chronic otitis externa (incl. furunculosis)
  - <u>Middle ear pathologies</u> - chronic suppurative otitis media (tubotympanic disease vs atticoantral disease), mastoiditis, fracture of temporal bone, CA middle ear (rare)

