# Chapter 2: Nose and paranasal sinuses

## Anatomy and physiology

## Symptoms, signs and investigations

- **Salient points of ENT Hx** - important to cover all possible nasal and sinus Sx:
  - Nasal obstruction
  - Nasal discharge
  - Sneezing
  - Facial pressure/ pain
  - Disorders of smell - Cacosmia, Ozaena, Hyposmia, Anosmia
  - Otological Sx - Hearing loss
  - Hallitosis
- **Nasal obstruction** - most common symptom in rhinology:
  - <u>Etiology of nasal obstruction</u> - anatomical, defects of mucosal lining, ANS:
    - **Anatomical deficits** - DNS, adenoidal hypertrophy, neoplasia, choanal atresia
    - **Defects of mucosal lining** - allergic and infective rhinitis, nasal polyps
    - **Autonomic nervous system** - vasomotor rhinitis (overactive parasympathetic nervous system causing vascular engorgement)
  - <u>Salient points of Hx</u>:
    - **Onset** - acute vs chronic
    - **Laterality** - unilateral, bilateral or alternating
    - **Progression** - progressive or variability in degree of obstruction:
      - <u>Progressive</u> - progressive unilateral nasal obstruction is suggestive of neoplasia
      - <u>Variability</u> - Viral infections (Coryza, influenza) resolves spontaneously over days, while allergic or vasoactive rhinitis have more variable presentation
    - **Timing/ Provacation** - exposure to allergens (pollens, animal danders), ambient temperature, humidity
- **Nasal discharge:**
  - <u>Terminology</u>:
    - **Rhinorrhoea** - runny nose causing discharge anteriorly
    - **Catarrh** - postnasal drip causing discharge passing backwards into basal pharynx
  - <u>Etiology of nasal discharge</u> - differentiated by colour:
    - **Watery/ mucoid** - rhinitis (viral, allergic, vasomotor), CSF leak
    - **Mucopurulent** - Bacterial rhinosinusitis, foreign body
    - **Serosanguinous** - Neoplasms
    - **Epistaxis** - local trauma, mass lesions, or systemic bleeding tendancies
- **Sneezing** - frequent accompinant of allergic or infective rhinitis
- **Facial pressure/ pain**
- **Otological Sx** - otalgia and hearing loss:
  - <u>Pathophysiology</u> - disorders of the nasal cavity and nasopharynx causing bloackade of the eustachian tube, resulting in OME
- **Disorders of smell:**
  - <u>Anosmia</u> - total loss of sense of smell
  - <u>Hyposmia</u> - reduced sense of smell
  - <u>Cacosmia</u> - unpleasant smell detected by others, principally caused by chronic nasal sepsis
  - <u>Ozaena</u> - foul offending smell detected by patients, esp. in anaerobic infections

## Allergic and vasomotor rhinitis

- **Definition of rhinitis** - inflammatory response of the mucous membrane of the nose, which may be intermittent or persistent
- **Etio-classification of rhinitis:**
  - <u>Allergic rhinitis</u> - seasonal allergens, perennial allergens, occupational allergens
  - <u>Infective rhinitis</u> - viral or bacterial infection
  - <u>Vasomotor rhinitis</u> - caused by autonomic nervous system abnormalities

  
  ![](img/classification_of_rhinitis.png)

### Allergic rhinitis

- **Definition** - symtpom complex caused by type 1 hypersensitivity reaction at the mucosal linings of the nasal cavity
- **Epidemiology** - \> 20% population of Western Europe have some degree of allergic rhinitis
- **Pathophysiology of allergic rhinitis:**
  - <u>Background of atopy</u> - Evidence of atopy such as asthma, eczema, allergic dermatitis, aspirin sensitivity etc.
  - <u>Exposure to allergens</u> - various allergens are implicated:
    - **Seasonal allergens** (hay fever) - pollens, grasses and weeds
    - **Perennial allergens** - house dust mites, feathers, annimal danders
    - **Ingested allergens** (concomittent GI Sx) - wheat, dairy products, nuts
  - <u>Mast cell degranulation</u> - binding of allergen to IgE on mast cells results in release of histamine, leukotrienes and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRSA)
- **Clinical features of allergic rhinitis:**
  - <u>Nasal obstruction</u> - bilateral nasal obstruction due to mucosal vasodilatation and oedema
  - <u>Rhinorrhoea</u> - watery nasal discharge due to enhanced activity of gland<sub>ular</sub> elements
  - <u>Sneezing</u> - paroxysms of sneezing due to mucosal stimulation
- **Signs of allergic rhinitis:**
  - <u>Anterior rhinoscopic findings</u>:
    - Nasal mucosa - boggy and oedematous in apperance, covered by a film of watery secretion
- **Ix and Dx evaluation** - clinical Dx but identification of allergen is important:
  - <u>Skin-prick test</u> - identify offending allergen to guide allergen avoidance
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>General measures</u> - allergen avoidance
    - <u>Desensitization</u> - only implicated if specific allergen is identified, but carries a risk of anaphylaxis
    - <u>Medical Tx</u> - topical and systemic drug therapy (e.g. mast cell stabilisers, H1 antagonists)
    - <u>Surgical Tx</u> - semi-effective for symptomatic relief only for patients refractory to medical Tx
  - **Allergen avoidance:**
    - <u>House-dust mites</u> - washing of bedclothes twice weekly (heat-sensitive allergen), abd avoid use of carpets and heavy curtains that collect house-dust mites in bedroom
    - <u>Animal dander</u> - keep pets outside of bedroom (+/- giving up the pet)
  - **Medical Tx:**
    - **Topical sodium cromoglycate:**
      - <u>MOA</u> - mast cell stabilisation to prevent release of vasoactive substances
      - <u>Efficacy</u> - less successful in allergic rhinitis than in asthma control
    - **Local decongestants** - sympathomimetics or steroids:
      - <u>Selection of topical sympathomimetics</u> - e.g. Ephedrine nose drops
      - <u>Selection of topical steroids</u> - Mometasone, fluticasone
      - <u>S/E</u> - rhinitis medicamentosa (rebound rhinitis) from discontinuation after prolonged use
    - **Systemic drug therapy** - oral antihistamines:
      - <u>MOA</u> - blockade of H1 receptors
      - <u>S/E</u> - sedation in 1st generation antihistamines
  - **Surgical Tx** - indicated only if refractory to medical therapy:
    - <u>Procedures</u> - turbinate resection, cautary or outfracture
    - <u>Efficacy</u> - effective for obstructive Sx, but rhinorrhoea and sneezing unaffected as local inflammation persists
- **Rhinitis medicamentosa:**
  - **Definition** - reactive vasodilatation of the nasal mucosa from discontinuation after prolonged use of local sympathomimetic decongestants
  - **Mx** - prophylactic:
    - <u>Prescription of local decongestants</u> - only prescribed as short, sharp courses of therapies **not intended for long term use**
    - <u>Drug substitution</u> - switch to steroid-based local decongestant, or systemic decongestant
    - <u>Partial turbinate resection</u> - if swelling becomes irreversible

### Vasomotor rhinitis

- **Definition** - nasal manifestations of rhinitis in the absence of allergic component, and is postulated to be related to nasal hypersensitivity due to autonomic nervous system dysfunction
- **Epidemiology** - common disease (10-15% of general population)
- **Pathophysiology of vasomotor rhinitis:**
  - <u>Autonomic dysfunction</u> - parasympathetic stimulation predominates over sympathetic stimulation in the nasal mucosa especially during changes in ambient temperatures and humidity
  - <u>Vasodilation</u> - causes oedematous mucosa (nasal congestion) and enhanced mucous secretion (rhinorrhoea)
- **Clinical features of vasomotor rhinitis:**
  - <u>Paroxysms of nasal Sx</u> - in relation to changes in ambient temperature, humidity, and metabolic states (e.g. hyperthyroidism, pregnancy, puberty and menopause)
  - <u>Classical triad of nasal Sx in rhinitis</u>:
    - **Nasal obstruction** - caused by congested mucosa particularly over the inferior turbinate causing complete obstruction of the airway
    - **Rhinorrhoea** - watery anterior nasal discharge due to enhanced mucus secretion
    - **Sneezing** - less prominent than in allergic rhinitis as there is fewer mucosal irritation
- **Signs of vasomotor rhinitis:**
  - <u>Anterior rhinoscopic findings</u> - Erythematous, oedematous inferior turbinate ocassionally completely blocking the airway
- **Mx:**
  - **Medical Tx** - local and systemic decongestants
  - **Surgical Tx** - submucosal diathermy, laser treatment, partial turbinectomy

## Nasal polyps

- **Definition** - bags of oedematous mucosa most frequently arising from the ethmoid cells and prolapse into the nasal cavity via the middle meatus
- **Pathophysiology** - not well understood:
  - <u>Predisposition in certain populations</u> - e.g. asthma, aspirin hypersensitivity, cystic fibrosis
  - <u>Systemic bilateral process</u> - nasal polyps always bilateral, unilaterality suggestive of neoplasia which requires Bx
- **Clinical features of nasal polyps:**
  - <u>Nasal obstruction</u> - often progressive, bilateral obstruction is the cardinal Sx
  - <u>Rhinorrhoea</u> - frequent and may be a/w ascending infections
  - <u>Disorders of smell</u> (Hyposmia and anosmia) - not uncommon
  - <u>Recurrent rhinosinusitis</u> - occurs if there is blockade of the ostiomeatal complex causing stasis in the dependent paranasal sinuses
  - <u>Otological Sx</u> (otalgia and hearing loss) - caused by eustachian tube obstruction resulting in OME
- **Signs of nasal polyps** - bilateral polyps found on endoscopy:
  - <u>Nasal endoscopic findings</u> - pale grey polypoidal mass that is mobile, and insensitive to palpation, and do not bleed (cf inferior turbinate hypertrophy)
  - <u>Indications for Bx</u> - unilaterality and features suggestive of haemorrhage
- **Ix and Dx** - Dx is purely clinical and addition Ix is ancillary:
  - <u>Nasal endoscopy</u>
  - <u>CT H&N</u> - radio-opacity in the paranasal sinuses, especially in the maxillary antrum
- **Mx:**
  - <u>Medical Tx</u> - short course of systemic steroids followed by long-term use of topical steroids
  - <u>Endoscopic ethmoidectomy</u> - for refractory or recurrent cases
  - <u>Mx of sinusitis</u> - appropriate medical and surgical Mx if sinusitis occurs
  - <u>Mx of associated comorbidities</u> - routine Mx of associated allergy or asthma
- **Antrochoanal polyp** - rare nasal polyp arising from the maxillary sinus:
  - <u>Epidemiology</u> - seen in young adults
  - <u>Pathology</u> - unilateral oedematous polyp in the maxillary sinus that prolapse via the posterior accessory ostium into nasal cavity and extends towards nasopharynx
  - <u>Clinical features</u> - unilateral nasal obstruction and otological Sx (if encrouching on eustachian tube)
  - <u>Mx</u> - pernasal excision with removal of cystic antral portion via antrostomy

