## Nasal septal pathologies and choanal atresia

### Deviated nasal septum

- Rare to find a septum completely lying along the midline
- **Etiology:**
  - Developmental abnormalities
  - Nasal trauma - recent or prior trauma to the nose (might not be able to recall)
- **Clinical features of deviated nasal septum** - not all are symptomatic:
  - <u>Nasal obstruction</u> - unilateral nasal obstruction, ocassionally bilateral nasal obstruction in S-shaped septal deflection
  - <u>Other nasal Sx</u> (e.g. facial pain, nasal discharge, epistaxis) - may be contributed by the septal deflection but reflects a more complicated pathology
- **Signs of DNS:**
  - <u>Rhinoscopic/ Endoscopic findings</u>:
    - Deviated nasal septum
    - Compensatory hypertrophy of the contralateral inferior turbinate
- **Mx** - surgical Mx only if symptomatic:
  - <u>Submucosous resection with septoplasty</u> - elevation of mucoperichondrial flaps on both sides and excision of defect and repositioning of septum
- **Complications of septoplasty:**
  - Nasal haemorrhage
  - Septal haematoma
  - Collapse of nasal dorsum (late feature due to excessive removal of septal cartilage)

### Septal haematoma

- **Definition** - haematoma formation due to haemorrhage into the subperichondrial space <u>caused by nasal trauma</u>
- **Clinical features of septal haematoma** - occurence after nasal trauma:
  - <u>Nasal obstruction</u> - sudden onset of severe nasal obstruction after nasal trauma
  - <u>Nasal tenderness</u> - only if haematoma becomes secondarily infected (causes cartilage necrosis and nose collapse)
- **Mx:**
  - Drainage of haematoma - via needle aspiration or formal incision and drainage
  - Packing - to prevent recurrence

