## Epistaxis

- **Definition** - nose-bleed, which is usually self-limiting, but can be massive and rapidly fatal
- **Common site of epistaxis** (90%) - Little's area (anterior portion of septum) containing Kisselbach's plexus (rich vascular anastomotic supply formed by end arteries)
- **Etiology of epistaxis** - classified into local and systemic:
  - Local pathologies:
    - Trauma - nasal trauma, nose-picking, foreign body
    - Infective - viral infection
    - Mass lesions - neoplasms of the nasal cavity and nasopharynx, juvenile angiofibroma
  - Systemic pathologies:
    - HTN
    - Drugs - e.g. anticoagulants, anti-platelets
    - Haematological diseases - thrombocytopenia (e.g. acute leukaemia), Haemophilia, Von Willebrand disease, other bleeding tendancies
    - Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu disease)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - Initial first aid measures
    - Assessment of blood loss
    - Evaluation of cause
    - Procedures to stop continued bleeding
  - **First aid measures** - Trotter's method:
    - <u>Pinch nostrils rather than nasal bridge</u> (continue respiration through mouth) - manual pressure over Little's area to control the bleeding
    - <u>Sitting position</u> - lower blood pressure by reducing venous return
    - <u>Lean forward</u> - avoid aspiration of blood
  - **Initial clinical assessment** - assessment of blood loss and resuscitation in cases of severe epistaxis:
    - <u>Vital signs and assessment of blood loss</u> - BP/P, pallor, sweating, mucosa etc.
    - <u>Initial resuscitation</u> - large bore IV canula and IV fluid resuscitation
    - <u>Initial Ix</u> - CBC, clotting profile, T&S
  - **Evaluation of site and cause of bleeding:**
    - Examination of the nasal cavity after epistaxis temporarily ceased - any abraision's over the Little's area, visualisation of neoplastic growth
    - Determine site of bleeding:
      - ICA territory - above middle turbinate
      - ECA territory - below middle turbinate
    - Haematological diseases - suggested on CBC and clotting profiles
  - **Control the bleeding** - depedent on whether bleeding is ceased and suspected site of bleeding:
    - <u>Silver nitrate cauterization or electrocauterisation</u> (after anaesthetic block) - if bleeding site is noted (e.g. Little's area)
    - <u>Anterior nasal packing</u> (if no clear bleeding point discernible) - ribbon gauze impregnated with bismuth iodoform paraffin paste (BIPP)
    - <u>Inflatable balloon tampanode</u> - alternative for anterior nasal packing
    - <u>Posterior nasal packing</u>
    - <u>Arterial ligation</u>

## Acute and chronic rhinosinusitis

- **Definition** - inflamatory process involving the lining of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses
- **Etiology** - rhinogenic in origin with predisposing factors:
  - <u>Prior infective rhinitis</u> - complicates with sinusitis as the lining of the nasal cavity is continuous with that of paranasal sinuses
  - <u>Anatomical defects involving middle meatus and ostiomeatal complex</u> - predisposes to disease of the major dependent sinuses (e.g. frontal, maxillary, and anterior sphenoid)
    - **Deviated nasal septum**
    - **Nasal polyposis**
    - **Turbinate hypertrophy**
    - **Nasal neoplasms**
- **Pathophysiology of acute rhinosinusitis:**
  - <u>Preceeding viral rhinosinusitis</u> - oedema causes blockade of the ostiomeatal complex and paralysis of mucocilliary escalator, resulting in retention of mucus
  - <u>Mucus stasis and secondary bacterial infection</u> - stasis of mucus in the sinuses will become secondarily infected by resident bacterial organisms in the nose
- **Clinical features of acute rhinosinusitis:**
  - **Prior infective rhinitis** - initially viral in nature but does not resolve
  - **Nasal symptoms:**
    - <u>Nasal obstruction</u>
    - <u>Nasal discharge</u> - mucopurulent in nature, which may be anterior or posterior
    - <u>Anosmia or hyposmia</u>
    - <u>Facial pressure/ pain</u> - worse and localised to sinuses upon bending forward
  - **Otological Sx** - reflects eustachian tube dysfunction:
    - Ear pain
    - Fullness
    - Hearing Loss
- **Dx evaluation of acute rhinosinusitis** - primarily a clinical diagnosis:
  - Dx is made when patient presents with:
    - 1\. Purulent nasal discharge (\< 4 weeks)
    - 2\. Nasal obstruction and/or facial pain/ pressure

